{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1147 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e4ce253828ee8a159a9ea197c5338a2845227f696a8ec3903f2cc235ae8d7547" :edb/db-before-t 1146 :edb/db-after-t 1147 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534459 50 #inst "2026-10-07T05:50:40.071Z" 13194139534459 true] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139534459 true] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/760/Source/Images/d295aff6e58a4512c5e355673a1589a0.png)" 13194139534459 false] [17592186082925 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^{3/2}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1 + [\\frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4 + 9x}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4 + 9x)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n$$\n\nto the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4+9x\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}∫_{4}^{16}u^{\\frac{1}{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}[\\frac{2}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}] - [(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([64] - [8]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{56}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 false] [17592186082925 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186082926 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186082926 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle \\int_{5}^{6}} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?\n\n1. $y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n2. $y= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n3. $y= \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" 13194139534459 false] [17592186082926 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ± ∫\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 16}}dx \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 16} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is\n\n$$\ny= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5\n$$" 13194139534459 false] [17592186082926 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083015 1100 "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]$?" 13194139534459 false] [17592186083015 1100 "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083015 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\arccos{x}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\ninto the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1 - x^{2} + 1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x^{2}}{1 - x^{2}}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 false] [17592186083015 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289374 13194139534459 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289378 13194139534459 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289381 13194139534459 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289384 13194139534459 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289395 13194139534459 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289409 13194139534459 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289416 13194139534459 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289423 13194139534459 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289430 13194139534459 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289360 13194139534459 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289367 13194139534459 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289388 13194139534459 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289402 13194139534459 true] [17592186083454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534459 true] [17592186083454 1100 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\ln (\\csc x)$ on $[1, 3]$?" 13194139534459 false] [17592186083454 1100 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)$ on $[1,3]?$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083454 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote, that we use $|\\operatorname{csc}⁡x|=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$, which is valid due to that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x>0$ on $[1,3].$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534459 true] [17592186083851 1100 "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2} - 4x}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?" 13194139534459 false] [17592186083851 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139534459 true] [17592186083851 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{x}^{2}-4x+1=(2x-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm (2x-1).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int (2x-1)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm ({x}^{2}-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y={x}^{2}-x+4.$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289361 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1089 "q-15323" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1100 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,2]?$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1113 #uuid "676c520e-2bd4-5a9f-a56a-7aedf1c83e6b" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289360 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289362 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289363 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289364 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289365 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289366 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1041 "selection" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289366 13194139534459 true] [17592186289361 1110 #uuid "c511cec6-3e20-558b-915d-99f55368802d" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289362 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534459 true] [17592186289362 1111 #uuid "0fac23b7-e567-57d0-bd41-68f565981d24" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289363 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534459 true] [17592186289363 1111 #uuid "38