{\\sqrt{2}}))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})]-[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289382 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289383 13194139534459 true] [17592186289382 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534459 true] [17592186289382 1041 "field-1" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289382 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289383 13194139534459 true] [17592186289382 1110 #uuid "674e8d2d-7ebb-5670-a1cb-802f3074ddab" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289383 1034 "\\frac{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534459 true] [17592186289383 1111 #uuid "9c7c219e-c126-57ee-bcda-4a9b40d1de4e" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289385 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1089 "q-362589" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1113 #uuid "e4fdd583-dc77-5faa-9875-6977242c4205" 13194139534459 true] [17592186289384 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ $a=-1,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1-2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2x}{1-{x}^{2}}